                <pb facs="0444" n="340" sameAs="frus:frus1932v03/pg340"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/4203: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai (Cunningham) to the
                        Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-02-15T18:00:00+08:00">February 15, 1932—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received February 15—12:55 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>56. Continuing my daily report number 50, February 14, 6 p.m.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>The situation along both sectors remains quiet. Shanghai awaits in fear
                        and trembling the beginning of the major Japanese drive to force the Chinese
                        back from Shanghai. This is expected some time during the next few days.
                        Intermittent artillery, machine-gun and rifle fire continues on both fronts.
                        It is now learned for the first time that on the 13th a small contingent of
                        Japanese troops pushed across the Woosung Creek but were forced to retreat
                        on account of deadly fire of Chinese defenders. Shells continue to fall in
                        the Settlement areas.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>As reported yesterday a force of Japanese troops landed at Wayside. They
                        appeared to be fully equipped and among their accoutrements were 5 large and
                        10 small tanks. This morning Lieutenant General Uyeda of the 9th Division
                        called on me.</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>Fresh beef continues to be scarce in Shanghai. Otherwise there is a
                        plentiful supply of foodstuffs on hand. Beef is on the way from other ports
                        which will relieve this shortage.</item>
                </list>
                <p>Repeated to the Legation for information.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Cunningham</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
